    {
      "label": "Instruction Fetch",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "instruction_fetch.rst",
      "source_location": "L3",
      "confidence_score": 1.0,
      "role": "section",
      "original_source": "D:\\MyWork\\verilog\\out\\ibex_mini_docs\\instruction_fetch.rst",
      "snippet": ".. _instruction-fetch:\n\nInstruction Fetch\n=================\n:file:`rtl/ibex_if_stage.sv.`\n\n.. figure:: images/if_stage.svg\n   :name: if_stage\n   :align: center",
      "id": "section_instruction_fetch_rst_3_instruction_fetch",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "instruction fetch"
    },
    {
      "label": "Branch Prediction",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "instruction_fetch.rst",
      "source_location": "L31",
      "confidence_score": 1.0,
      "role": "section",
      "original_source": "D:\\MyWork\\verilog\\out\\ibex_mini_docs\\instruction_fetch.rst",
      "snippet": "If Ibex has been configured with an instruction cache (parameter ICache == 1), then the prefetch buffer is replaced by the icache module (:ref:`icache`).\nThe interfaces of the icache module are the same as the prefetch buffer with two additions.\nFirstly, a signal to enable the cache which is driven from a custom CSR.\nSecondly a signal to the flush the cache which is set every time a ``fence.i`` instruction is executed.\n\n.. _branch-prediction:\n\nBranch Prediction\n-----------------\n\nIbex can be configured to use static branch prediction by setting the ``BranchPrediction`` parameter to 1.\nThis improves performance by predicting that any branch with a negative offset is taken and that any branch with a positive offset is not.\nWhen successful, the prediction removes a stall cycle from a taken branch.\nHowever, there is a mis-predict penalty if a branch is wrongly predicted to be taken.\nThis penalty is at least one cycle, or at least two cycles if the instruction following the branch is uncompressed and not aligned.",
      "id": "section_instruction_fetch_rst_31_branch_prediction",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "branch prediction"
    },
    {
      "label": "Instruction-Side Memory Interface",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "instruction_fetch.rst",
      "source_location": "L41",
      "confidence_score": 1.0,
      "role": "section",
      "original_source": "D:\\MyWork\\verilog\\out\\ibex_mini_docs\\instruction_fetch.rst",
      "snippet": "Ibex can be configured to use static branch prediction by setting the ``BranchPrediction`` parameter to 1.\nThis improves performance by predicting that any branch with a negative offset is taken and that any branch with a positive offset is not.\nWhen successful, the prediction removes a stall cycle from a taken branch.\nHowever, there is a mis-predict penalty if a branch is wrongly predicted to be taken.\nThis penalty is at least one cycle, or at least two cycles if the instruction following the branch is uncompressed and not aligned.\nThis feature is *EXPERIMENTAL* and its effects are not yet fully documented.\n\nInstruction-Side Memory Interface\n---------------------------------\n\nThe following table describes the signals that are used to fetch instructions.\nThis interface is a simplified version of the interface used on the data interface as described in :ref:`load-store-unit`.\nThe main difference is that the instruction interface does not allow for write transactions and thus needs less signals.\n\n.. tabularcolumns:: |p{4cm}|l|p{9cm}|",
      "id": "section_instruction_fetch_rst_41_instruction_side_memory_interface",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "instruction-side memory interface"
    },
    {
      "label": "Misaligned Accesses",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "instruction_fetch.rst",
      "source_location": "L74",
      "confidence_score": 1.0,
      "role": "section",
      "original_source": "D:\\MyWork\\verilog\\out\\ibex_mini_docs\\instruction_fetch.rst",
      "snippet": "| ``instr_rdata_i[31:0]``     | input     | Data read from memory                         |\n+-----------------------------+-----------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| ``instr_rdata_intg_i[6:0]`` | input     | Data integrity bits from memory               |\n+-----------------------------+-----------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| ``instr_err_i``             | input     | Memory access error                           |\n+-----------------------------+-----------+-----------------------------------------------+\n\n\nMisaligned Accesses\n-------------------\n\nExternally, the IF interface performs word-aligned instruction fetches only.\nMisaligned instruction fetches are handled by performing two separate word-aligned instruction fetches.\nInternally, the core can deal with both word- and half-word-aligned instruction addresses to support compressed instructions.\nThe LSB of the instruction address is ignored internally.",
      "id": "section_instruction_fetch_rst_74_misaligned_accesses",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "misaligned accesses"
    },
    {
      "label": "Protocol",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "instruction_fetch.rst",
      "source_location": "L83",
      "confidence_score": 1.0,
      "role": "section",
      "original_source": "D:\\MyWork\\verilog\\out\\ibex_mini_docs\\instruction_fetch.rst",
      "snippet": "-------------------\n\nExternally, the IF interface performs word-aligned instruction fetches only.\nMisaligned instruction fetches are handled by performing two separate word-aligned instruction fetches.\nInternally, the core can deal with both word- and half-word-aligned instruction addresses to support compressed instructions.\nThe LSB of the instruction address is ignored internally.\n\n\nProtocol\n--------\n\nThe protocol used to communicate with the instruction cache or the instruction memory is very similar to the protocol used by the LSU on the data interface of Ibex.\nSee the description of the LSU in :ref:`LSU Protocol<lsu-protocol>` for details about this protocol.",
      "id": "section_instruction_fetch_rst_83_protocol",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "protocol"
    },
